            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Smooths an image with a Gaussian blur.\n\nBLUR IS A CHOICE ABOUT SCALE:\n'sigma' is how far the smoothing reaches, and that is really a decision about what counts as detail and what counts as noise. 'kernel size' is just the window the arithmetic happens in; it needs to be comfortably wider than sigma - about six times sigma covers the whole curve - or the blur is cut off at the edge of its own window. Sigma is the dial you want.\n\nSigma is clamped above zero: set it to 0 or below and it becomes 0.1, and the widget is corrected to show that - a blur of nothing is not a blur, and it will not pretend otherwise.\n\nThe defaults, sigma 0.5 in a 9-wide window, are a very light blur.\n\nIT HAS A k. TWIN:\nk.gaussian_blur does the same job and offers larger windows (up to 29 against 19 here). The difference that matters: tv.gaussian_blur keeps whole-number images as whole numbers (0..255 in, 0..255 out), while k.gaussian_blur converts to floats first. If an image arrives from a camera or a file as integers and you want it to stay that way, use this one. If the next step is an edge or blob filter, you are in kornia's territory anyway, and staying in one family keeps the assumptions consistent.\n\nSYNTAX:\ntv.gaussian_blur\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe image. Receiving it does the work. Each channel is blurred separately.\n\nsigma:\nHow far the smoothing reaches, in pixels. Default 0.5.\n\nkernel size:\nThe width of the window, 3 to 19, odd numbers only. Default 9.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe blurred image, the same shape, channels first, in the dtype you sent.\n\nCHANNELS FIRST, AND 0 TO 1:\nThese want images as (channels, height, width) - (3, H, W) for colour, (1, H, W) for grayscale. Images from cameras and files usually arrive the other way round, (H, W, 3), so the nodes guess: a small last dimension and a large third-from-last means height-width-channels, and they transpose it for you. The guess is right except on very small images, where those two tests stop telling you anything.\n\nUnlike the k. nodes, these are happy with whole-number images: hand them 0..255 integers and you get 0..255 integers back, correctly scaled. Floats are taken to run 0 to 1.\n",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 800,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 680,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Smooths an image with a Gaussian blur.\n\nBLUR IS A CHOICE ABOUT SCALE:\n'sigma' is how far the smoothing reaches, and that is really a decision about what counts as detail and what counts as noise. 'kernel size' is just the window the arithmetic happens in; it needs to be comfortably wider than sigma - about six times sigma covers the whole curve - or the blur is cut off at the edge of its own window. Sigma is the dial you want.\n\nSigma is clamped above zero: set it to 0 or below and it becomes 0.1, and the widget is corrected to show that - a blur of nothing is not a blur, and it will not pretend otherwise.\n\nThe defaults, sigma 0.5 in a 9-wide window, are a very light blur.\n\nIT HAS A k. TWIN:\nk.gaussian_blur does the same job and offers larger windows (up to 29 against 19 here). The difference that matters: tv.gaussian_blur keeps whole-number images as whole numbers (0..255 in, 0..255 out), while k.gaussian_blur converts to floats first. If an image arrives from a camera or a file as integers and you want it to stay that way, use this one. If the next step is an edge or blob filter, you are in kornia's territory anyway, and staying in one family keeps the assumptions consistent.\n\nSYNTAX:\ntv.gaussian_blur\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe image. Receiving it does the work. Each channel is blurred separately.\n\nsigma:\nHow far the smoothing reaches, in pixels. Default 0.5.\n\nkernel size:\nThe width of the window, 3 to 19, odd numbers only. Default 9.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe blurred image, the same shape, channels first, in the dtype you sent.\n\nCHANNELS FIRST, AND 0 TO 1:\nThese want images as (channels, height, width) - (3, H, W) for colour, (1, H, W) for grayscale. Images from cameras and files usually arrive the other way round, (H, W, 3), so the nodes guess: a small last dimension and a large third-from-last means height-width-channels, and they transpose it for you. The guess is right except on very small images, where those two tests stop telling you anything.\n\nUnlike the k. nodes, these are happy with whole-number images: hand them 0..255 integers and you get 0..255 integers back, correctly scaled. Floats are taken to run 0 to 1.\n"
        },
